        "math_academy_id": "q-316995",
        "problem": "The system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x=6 \\\\ 2x+z=2 \\\\ 3x+4y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |  |  | $x$ |  |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |  |  | $y$ |  | $=$ |  | $2$ |  | . |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-3}} | $-2$ |  |  |  | $z$ |  |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+0y+0z=6 \\\\ 2x+0y+z=2 \\\\ 3x+4y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
